00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.280 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome to the program  today Ellen rupal she'll who is a  correspondent for the Atlantic also  author of the job work and its future in  a time of radical change  Ellen it's so great to have you on  thanks for having me David it's so to  begin with we've been talking sort of  piecemeal for many years about  technological automation the  possibilities of universal basic income  examining the areas of our  socio-economic system that are working  well in the areas that that are not  working so well but it seems that often  missing from this conversation is the  reality that at a certain point humans  are going to kind of need to re-examine  the relationship to work and the idea of  a sort of 40-hour full-time job as the  sole path towards other than independent  wealth sustaining oneself in the world  can you talk a little bit about sort of  the big picture of the change that you  see happening in terms of humans and  their relationship to work David you put  that beautifully okay and what you just  described is precisely why I wrote this  book my concern going in here was that  we were in something like a denial  national denial about how things were  changing economically socially on all  different kind of all different levels  and what we were doing was desperately  trying to hang on to jobs that were no  longer salient in the current economy  right jobs that that would were being  outsourced jobs increasingly that are  being automated and we weren't really  coming to grips with the fact that jobs  per se might be on the way out okay  now historically jobs are kind of a new  idea I mean it's hard to imagine this  about before about 1900 or so most  people didn't really have jobs right  most Americans worked in agriculture  they either  on their land or worked on farms many  also have trades and were entrepreneurs  of a sort so the whole notion of having  a job was not really necessarily a  positive thing as you can imagine  there's an agricultural community if you  had a job that means you would be  holding to a landowner you were say a  farmhand that was your job but what you  really aspired to have was your own land  and be something of an entrepreneur so  the whole notion of having a job has  really only since the late Industrial  Revolution and you know going forward  into our current century obviously that  21st century has held such cachet and  we're hanging onto this idea that we all  need to be working for someone else  right and we need to be employed in a  certain kind of way and all this to me  seems a little nostalgic and a little a  historical because in fact that's not  the way it's always been  and going forward you know that may not  be the way it's going to be and we need  to come to grips with that and  understand what steps we need to take to  ensure that everyone is able to afford  to live is able to make a living and  also is able to find a sense of purpose  and meaning in their work so you talked  about the discussion of bringing jobs  back that don't exist anymore  and this has become extraordinarily  politicized and what I've pointed out is  that if you have two presidential  candidates for example and they argue  about who is going to bring back for  example manufacturing jobs from Asia or  whatever the discussion may be as soon  as one of them says the truth which is a  lot of those jobs just don't exist  anymore their opponent says that's a  defeatist attitude and that's exactly  why you should vote for me instead of my  opponent so how can we break through  that and actually start to think about  what we're going to do in reality  instead of fantasizing about bringing  jobs back that don't exist when there's  no political incentive to do it based on  how our election system works  right again a very good point um you  know jobs many jobs can go anywhere you  know they don't really have a  natural-born home you know we think that  they do a lot of politicians say that  they do but they actually don't right  they can go anywhere and not just  factory jobs but white-collar jobs  knowledge jobs coding jobs for example  this is a very vaunted category of jobs  we are with there's a lot of talk about  having former factory workers or coal  miners learn coding but that's a  temporary fix at best because the  ability to code is an international one  there are people all over the world who  are fully capable of doing it it's not  necessarily an American job right so the  notion that we can keep jobs of all  sorts in the United States in the  digital age is pretty naive and you're  absolutely right that when political  candidates talk straight on this on this  point they often lose traction but I  think I traveled the United States to do  this book and in areas for example the  Rust Belt or in Kentucky in the mining  in areas of Kentucky the the residents  are actually quite aware of this you  know people are not stupid  they know what's going on  so speaking the truth on this I think is  getting to be going to be more and more  accepted because people are  understanding it so even though you know  we're sometimes told for example the  marine effort right where a Chinese  manufacturer was setting up in Ohio  outside of Dayton in a former GM plant I  don't know if you were to call that ones  in the recent news and in fact guess  what they're not really going to do  manufacturing there there they pulled  back dramatically on the number of hires  they're going to make in Ohio right so  this is you know a more most recent case  of this  it's of course people there now  understand that this is not something  that's going to have any durability over  the long term and I think increasingly  politicians who speak this truth will be  accepted what else in your mind counts  as a temporary solution and I guess what  I'm what I'm interested in hearing about  a little bit is universal basic income  which I've been studying for a very long  time and while I see it heavily  supported by lots of progressive  economists most of them are saying I  support it as a step based on where we  are right now but it's not really a  long-term solution to the problems that  we're going to have yeah universal basic  income is is something that I was thrown  about quite a bit recently Silicon  Valley Valley got on board with that of  course because they in recent years have  started to get to admit that they are on  unemployment and they were concerned  about that so the whole idea of you bei  and versatile basic income was very  tantalizing to people in Silicon Valley  I described it in the book as something  that should be used as a spice now how  does a main course okay so there are  examples for example in Canada in the  1970s this basic income was applied to a  community in Canada and it was very very  helpful okay so it was an impoverished  community or poor community and they  applied it selectively over a short term  a few years and it did lift people out  of poverty it gave them the opportunity  to become more entrepreneurial for  example in one case I interviewed some  of the people who had actually been  living there in the 1970s so for example  one guy was able to buy a truck right  and and able to be much more effective  in his agricultural efforts because he'd  had this income boost  young men were able to stay in high  school and graduate from high school  young women or young mothers were able  to stay home with their weather infants  these were all positive things and so I  believe a universal basic income or a  basic income guarantee as it's sometimes  called taking out the word univer  whistle can be very helpful applied  selectively but of course I don't think  anyone believes it's a long-term  solution nationally it's extremely  expensive highly controversial and it  doesn't solve things over the long term  when we think about the class impact of  what's coming there are is one point of  view which says ok technological  unemployment or automation related  unemployment is going to  disproportionately affect people who are  already economically disadvantaged way  more than people who are upper middle  class and higher however a bunch of  reports that I've looked at which  estimate that anywhere between 40 and 60  percent of current jobs would be subject  to partial or full automation a lot of  those jobs are what I guess we would  call white-collar jobs or information  jobs so what is your assessment of the  truth about what the class impact will  be of this well again quite a student  observation David and in comment over  the I think it's not well known that  since about 2000 the demand for skill in  the United States has actually declined  okay now that's a hard thing to get your  head around because what we're told is  we need more and more education to be  competitive that more and more of us  need to graduate from college or  graduate school but in fact the demand  for skill has actually declined since  2000 it increased before that time and  therefore more and more education and  specialized training was was effective  before that but since 2000 not so much  and it's because of what you're  describing the middle skill jobs have  been hollowed out to a great degree and  meanwhile I spoke to oh my god dozens of  computer scientists who told me that the  kind of jobs that are most desired to be  automated are the most skilled jobs  right those are the highest paying jobs  those are the kinds of jobs that  employers what  love to see automated if they could be  automated let me give you an example  there are machines that can flip  hamburgers right now right  but fast-food outlets are not  necessarily buying those machines  because they can pay people very little  to flip hamburgers right so they don't  want to buy machines and machines are  more expensive than the people and so  why bother but if you can automate a  highly sophisticated financial advisors  job or a physicians job or a lawyer's  job the payoff is enormous so there's a  tremendous rush to see how those  particular jobs can be automated so I  think it's really naive to think that  it's only low-skilled jobs that are  becoming digitalized those were the  easiest ones to do and of course many of  them have been but the focus now is  going up the pay scale and going up the  skill scale so this is going to be kind  of a less class-based than you may think  many people are concerned about this and  are preparing for this and Millennials  and Generation Z you know people under  you know age twenty eight twenty five  are finding themselves in many cases  underemployed even though they have  college degrees they're finding that  they cannot find positions commiserate  with their education so this is  something you know we need to deal with  an effective way and something again  that we have to face rather than telling  everyone they need more and more  education and more and more skills we  have to sit back and think okay what  kind of skills what kind of education  and what kind of jobs the book is  fantastic it's called the job work and  it's future and a time of radical change  it really ties together a lot of the  topics we've been talking about on the  program for a long time we've been  speaking with the book's author Ellen  rupal shell who is also a correspondent  for the Atlantic Ellen really a pleasure  speaking to you today thank you so much  David it was a real pleasure

